From 60589fdae143927496903d620f054766871f6dee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:56:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: make the GPU programs of a native Dolby Vision
 session ready before its first frame

libplacebo makes the GPU program of a way of drawing when a frame first needs it, and the bridge
draws the frames of a stream more than one way: directly when nothing is over the picture,
composed when the GUI, a subtitle or a capture is (and while the two ways are compared), at
another fidelity step-down level when the GPU falls behind, and without the residual of the
enhancement layer while that resynchronizes after a seek. Each first such frame waited for the
compile of its programs. Measured on the software bridge (zink on lavapipe, its own shader cache
off), with the composer of ETSI GS CCM 001: the first frame with the GUI over a picture drawn
directly took 201 ms instead of 45, the first at level 1 95 instead of 65, the first without the
layer 270 instead of 21, the first at level 2 336 instead of 45 (one or two programs each). On a
GPU with little time to spare that is a late frame, and it comes when the GUI appears, or when the
GPU is already behind.

dvbridge_render_warm() draws the first frame of a native output, before it is prepared, every way
the session can draw it, into the renderer's own targets: composed and, where the GPU can, directly;
at every step-down level that the frames can be drawn at (the one that advancedsettings.xml forces;
else, where the setting lets Kodi step down, every level that changes anything for the stream;
else level 0), the level in use first, then the nearest; with the residual of the layer and
without it (without one, every level above 1 draws what level 1 draws, which is made once). Each
render is waited for; renders are made while less than 1000 ms have passed, the others left for
the frame that first needs them. With the program cache of earlier sessions (2ecefa4) the programs
come from it, and what the warm-up costs is the GPU time of its renders.

Nothing that is presented changes. The renders go to the renderer's picture and to the window's
back buffer, which the frame then writes whole, and the renderer's state is given back: its
candidate, transport state, notes, level and what it has found of the bars written first;
libplacebo's errors are reset, and its picture is taken as not clean (it is cleared once more where
the next picture does not fill it). Measured on the software bridge with five frames as a session
draws them (directly, composed with the GUI, composed at level 1, directly without the layer,
composed at level 2): the same transport bytes, frame by frame, with and without the warm-up; no
program compiled at any of the five after it (without it: 6, 1, 1, 2 and 2; with libplacebo's
reshaping, dvccmcomposer off, 4, 1, 1, 1 and 1, and none either after it). The warm-up made 12
renders and compiled 14 programs, in 2.1 s on the software renderer (more than the 1000 ms that
Kodi gives it: there the rest would be left for the frames); a later session that started with
the cache filled by it compiled none (14 taken from it), in 0.56 s.

The first frame of a native output is not told to the fidelity step-down as a presentation (the
warm-up delays it; its GPU time is its own, the warm-up comes before the timer begins), and its
preparation time leaves the warm-up out. Logged once in each native output: how many ways were made
ready, in how long, how many programs were compiled. <video><dvwarmup>false</dvwarmup></video> in
advancedsettings.xml turns it off.

Not warmed: the reconstruction for output as HDR10. The GUI goes over it in Kodi's own HDR path and
it has no step-down levels, so its programs do not change with them; the only other one that a
video can need is that of a frame without the residual of its layer (after a seek), made by the
frame that first needs it.

The idea of compiling the variants of the overlay path before playback is that of the YBLOD fork
(dv_source_renderer_prepare_overlays()); here the bridge's own frames are drawn, with libplacebo's
programs.

With the expert settings of native output (CDVEngineSettings): a level
set in videoscreen.hadesdvstepmode is the one the frames are drawn at,
as one that advancedsettings.xml forces, and "never" leaves level 0
alone; with videoscreen.hadesdvpathmode set to always composed no frame
is drawn directly, and the direct way is not made ready. The composer is
the one that the settings gave the bridge when the output began.
